<GuideLayout slug="what-is-an-arborist">
  <p>
    An arborist is a specialist in the care of individual trees: how they grow, what makes them sick, and when they
    become dangerous. A tree removal crew is very good at taking trees down safely. An arborist helps you decide whether
    a tree needs to come down at all, and what to do if it doesn't.
  </p>

  <h2>What does "certified" mean?</h2>
  <p>
    The most widely recognized credential is ISA Certified Arborist, from the International Society of Arboriculture.
    To earn it, an arborist needs several years of experience in tree care and must pass an exam covering tree biology,
    diagnosis, pruning, safety and risk assessment, and then keep up with continuing education. You can check an
    arborist's certification on the ISA website.
  </p>

  <h2>What an arborist does</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tree risk assessment:</strong> judging how likely a tree is to fail, and what it would hit if it did.</li>
    <li><strong>Diagnosis:</strong> identifying disease, pests, decay and root problems.</li>
    <li><strong>Pruning plans:</strong> deciding what to cut so a tree stays healthy and strong.</li>
    <li><strong>Documentation:</strong> written reports for permits, HOAs, insurance and the state exemption for dangerous trees.</li>
    <li><strong>Construction advice:</strong> protecting valuable trees during building or renovation work.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to call an arborist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>You're not sure whether a tree is dangerous or just looks bad.</li>
    <li>A valuable or large tree shows cracks, decay, fungus or a new lean.</li>
    <li>You need written documentation for a permit, the state exemption, an HOA or an insurance claim.</li>
    <li>A tree's leaves are thinning, yellowing or dropping out of season.</li>
    <li>You're planning construction near a tree you'd like to keep.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Can an arborist save you money?</h2>
  <p>
    Often, yes. An assessment may show that careful pruning is enough and the tree can stay. And when a tree really is
    dangerous, an arborist's written assessment can, under Florida Statute 163.045, exempt its removal on residential
    property from local permit fees and replacement requirements. Read more in our
    <a href="/tree-care-guides/do-i-need-a-permit-to-remove-a-tree/">permit guide</a>.
  </p>

